-- ── THE PAUSE-REASON VOCABULARY GAINS 'onboarding' (D-07) ───────────────────
--
-- DROP-THEN-ADD, following 0017 exactly. `CADENCE_PAUSE_REASONS` in
-- src/db/schema/cadences.ts is a TypeScript const with no `check()` beside it —
-- the constraint is hand-written in 0017 — so drizzle-kit cannot see this change
-- and emitted nothing for it. Widening it here is what stops the 05-03 write
-- from raising 23514 against a constraint nobody remembered existed.
--
-- WIDENING ONLY. Nothing sets 'onboarding' yet; the behavioural arms (who pauses,
-- and what lifts it) land in plan 05-03. A CHECK that admits a value no writer
-- produces is inert; a writer producing a value no CHECK admits is an outage.

ALTER TABLE public.cadences DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS cadences_pause_reason_known;
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE public.cadences
  ADD CONSTRAINT cadences_pause_reason_known
  CHECK (pause_reason IS NULL OR pause_reason IN ('customer_reply', 'client_paused', 'onboarding'));
--> statement-breakpoint

-- ── TABLE PRIVILEGES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- The sixth restatement of 0003's rule. drizzle-kit models tables and policies;
-- it does NOT model GRANTs, and Postgres checks PRIVILEGES BEFORE ROW-LEVEL
-- SECURITY — so a table with a perfect policy and no grant returns `42501
-- permission denied`, an ERROR rather than an empty result, which leaks the
-- table's existence and breaks the property that denial and absence are
-- indistinguishable.
--
-- REVOKE-THEN-GRANT, following 0005/0007/0011/0017/0025. Hosted Supabase runs
-- ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES at bootstrap and silently hands anon and
-- authenticated the full set — including TRUNCATE, WHICH IS NOT SUBJECT TO RLS,
-- so no policy test could ever catch it. A fresh `supabase start` database (what
-- CI runs) has no such default, so skipping the REVOKE makes CI and production
-- disagree, with production being the permissive one.

REVOKE ALL ON public.message_templates FROM anon;
--> statement-breakpoint
REVOKE ALL ON public.message_templates FROM authenticated;
--> statement-breakpoint
REVOKE ALL ON public.touch_drafts FROM anon;
--> statement-breakpoint
REVOKE ALL ON public.touch_drafts FROM authenticated;
--> statement-breakpoint

-- READ-ONLY TO `authenticated` — BOTH tables, for two different reasons.
--
-- `message_templates` is the words that go out in a tradie's name, and inserting
-- a version is one step of a decision whose other step is attesting to it (D-06).
-- The console's template editor is a server action, exactly like every D-16
-- control in 0017: a console role holding a direct INSERT grant is a console
-- role holding the ability to perform half of that decision, leaving a template
-- that is live and unattested. The write-once approval columns are only
-- write-once if one writer holds the pen.
--
-- `touch_drafts` is COMPLIANCE AND AUDIT EVIDENCE (AI-04), and the argument 0025
-- makes for `inbound_messages` applies here with the direction reversed. There,
-- the record answers "did we honour that STOP". Here it answers "what did the
-- model write, how many attempts did it take, and how much did the human change
-- before approving" — the measurement V2-01 is gated on. A console-writable
-- INSERT path is a path by which that measurement can be fabricated after the
-- fact; a DELETE grant removes the record of what was sent in a client's name.
--
-- The approve write is likewise service-role: `status = 'approved'` is the ONE
-- status dispatch will send, and the unique partial index that makes at most one
-- of them exist per touch is only a guarantee if the writes go through one path.
--
-- anon gets SELECT on both so an unauthenticated read returns `[]` rather than
-- 42501; the policies above then return zero rows, because `has_tenant_access()`
-- admits nothing for an anonymous JWT.
GRANT SELECT ON public.message_templates TO anon;
--> statement-breakpoint
GRANT SELECT ON public.message_templates TO authenticated;
--> statement-breakpoint
GRANT ALL ON public.message_templates TO service_role;
--> statement-breakpoint
GRANT SELECT ON public.touch_drafts TO anon;
--> statement-breakpoint
GRANT SELECT ON public.touch_drafts TO authenticated;
--> statement-breakpoint
GRANT ALL ON public.touch_drafts TO service_role;